title: The Schlageter Speech
subtitle: Radek praises a dead nationalist saboteur before the International.
new-page: true
tags: rf_event
view-if: year = 1923 and month >= 7
max-visits: 1

= The Schlageter Speech

On 20 June, at a session of the <span class="p-comintern">Comintern</span>'s Executive, Karl Radek gave a speech on Albert Leo Schlageter, a Freikorps veteran shot by the French for sabotage in the Ruhr. Radek called him a "wanderer into the void" and invited German nationalists to find their way to the workers' side against the Entente.

Moscow expects the party to follow this "Schlageter course". Communist speakers could debate with nationalist students and völkisch groups. Many members are confused.

- @amplify
- @tolerate
- @oppose

@amplify
title: Take the course seriously: debate the nationalists and win their youth.
subtitle: Pleases Moscow, <span class="f-stalin">Thälmann</span> and the <span class="f-right">Right</span>. Angers the <span class="f-ultra">Ultra-Left</span> and the <span class="p-spd">SPD</span>.
on-arrival: {!
window.RF.obey(Q, 6);
window.RF.lean(Q, {right: 2});
Q.members += 8;
Q.spd_relation -= 6;
Q.dnvp_relation += 4;
Q.nsdap_relation += 3;
!}

Communist and nationalist speakers share platforms. A few young nationalists join the party. The <span class="p-spd">SPD</span> press is delighted to quote the *Rote Fahne* next to the völkisch papers.

@tolerate
title: Repeat the line in the paper but keep it away from the factories.
subtitle: Little effect either way.
on-arrival: {!
Q.members += 3;
Q.spd_relation -= 2;
!}

The party prints the speeches and goes on with its factory work. The episode passes.

@oppose
title: Refuse: a workers' party cannot borrow the nationalists' heroes.
subtitle: Defies Moscow. Pleases the <span class="f-ultra">Ultra-Left</span> and <span class="f-left">Left</span>. Angers <span class="f-stalin">Thälmann</span>.
on-arrival: {!
window.RF.defy(Q, 8);
window.RF.lean(Q, {left: 2});
Q.spd_relation += 3;
!}

The party declines to follow the course. Radek takes it personally, and Moscow notes who in Germany thinks for themselves.
